## 11. Workflow reference (lifecycle playbooks)

### 11.1 Decision matrix: which mode to use

| Situation | Recommended approach | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| Quick code question | Interactive `claude` (no spec) | Low coordination cost. |
| Initial exploration or brainstorming | Plan mode | Explore without editing or formalizing too early. |
| Feature with clear product behavior | Spec, requirements-first | Ensures requirements, acceptance, and traceability. |
| Feature driven by architecture, NFRs, or constraints | Spec, design-first | Starts from the technical design and derives feasible requirements. |
| Well-understood, low-risk feature | Quick spec (`sdd-spec-quick`) | Generates requirements/design/tasks in one pass. |
| Complex or critical bug | Bugfix spec | Focuses on root cause, expected behavior, and non-regression. |
| Broad refactoring | Spec, design-first | Requires plan, dependencies, and acceptance criteria. |
| PR review in CI | Headless `claude -p` | Automates review without an interactive terminal. |
| Multi-repo / PR work | `claude` CLI + `gh` | Branch protection and required CI still apply. |
| Integration with external tooling | MCP | Gives the agent controlled access to tools/context. |
| Packaged knowledge of a technology/process | Plugin | Bundles commands, agents, skills, hooks, and MCP servers. |
| Repetitive task on an editor/agent event | Hook | Automates with triggers and reduces manual variation. |

### 11.2 New feature, single spec (standard flow)

```text
1. /sdd-discovery "<idea>"                # routes, writes brief.md
2. /sdd-spec-init <feature-name>          # creates specs/<feature-name>/
3. /sdd-spec-requirements <feature-name>  # drafts requirements.md  [human approval]
4. /sdd-spec-design <feature-name>        # drafts design.md (+ research.md)  [human approval]
5. /sdd-spec-tasks <feature-name>         # drafts tasks.md (test-first pairing)  [human approval]
6. /sdd-impl <feature-name>               # autonomous execution, TDD inside (red→green→refactor)
7. /sdd-validate-impl <feature-name>      # final validation gate
```

### 11.3 Brownfield extension

```text
1. /sdd-steering                          # capture current project memory
2. /sdd-discovery "<idea>"                # routes the work
3. /sdd-spec-init <feature-name>
4. /sdd-spec-requirements <feature-name>
5. /sdd-validate-gap <feature-name>       # check requirements vs existing code
6. /sdd-spec-design <feature-name>
7. /sdd-validate-design <feature-name>    # optional review gate
8. /sdd-spec-tasks <feature-name>
9. /sdd-impl <feature-name>
```

### 11.4 Multi-spec initiative

```text
1. /sdd-discovery "<large idea>"          # writes brief.md and roadmap.md
2. /sdd-spec-batch                        # parallel multi-spec creation
                                          # cross-spec review for contradictions
3. /sdd-impl <feature-1>                  # per-spec implementation
   /sdd-impl <feature-2>
```

### 11.5 Bugfix flow

```text
1. Reproduce the problem and record evidence.
2. Create a Bugfix Spec (bugfix.md with Current / Expected / Unchanged Behavior).
3. Ask the agent to investigate the root cause before proposing a patch.
4. Review design.md with the root cause and fix approach.
5. Write a regression test that FAILS before the fix (red) and PASSES after (green).
6. Add a non-regression test for unchanged behavior.
7. Submit a small PR with an explicit root cause explanation.
```

### 11.6 Refactor flow

```text
1. Define the goal: readability, performance, modularity, debt removal, migration.
2. List behaviors that must not change; ensure they are covered by green tests first.
3. Use design-first if the refactoring is structural.
4. Break the work into small phases.
5. Keep the suite green at every phase (refactor only under green).
6. Do NOT mix refactoring with functional changes in the same PR.
```

### 11.7 Small change, no spec

```text
1. /sdd-discovery "<change>"              # returns: "no spec needed, implement directly"
2. <direct implementation, still test-first where it makes sense>
```

`/sdd-discovery` is allowed to return "no spec needed" as a valid route. SDD is not about putting a formal contract around every change — only those where the contract earns its cost.

### 11.8 Quick spec policy

The quick flow (`sdd-spec-quick`) still produces the same artifacts (requirements, design, tasks), but without approvals between phases. It SHALL NOT be used for:

- Regulated or sensitive domains.
- Changes with financial, security, privacy, or availability impact.
- Architectural changes.
- Features with ambiguous requirements.
- Critical bugs.
- Flows that require cross-team alignment.

### 11.9 Upstream & downstream workflows (`wf:product/discovery` → `wf:development`)

`csdd init` ships two BMAD-style orchestrator workflows that bracket the single-spec flow above. Each is a sub-agent (the lead) that dispatches a set of phase skills; the seam between them is a normal, validated csdd spec, so no gate is bypassed.

Upstream — `wf-product-discovery` (decide *what & why*):

```text
1. discovery-product-brief   # docs/product/product-brief.md   (optional, anchors the rest)
2. discovery-research        # docs/product/research/*.md       (optional, evidence-graded)
3. discovery-prfaq           # docs/product/prfaq.md            (optional, stress-test value)
4. discovery-prd             # docs/product/prd.md              (always — features with FR-N)
5. discovery-ux-spec         # docs/product/ux/DESIGN.md + EXPERIENCE.md (if UI)
6. discovery-handoff         # BRIDGE: steering update + csdd spec init +
                             #         FR-N → EARS requirements [csdd spec validate/approve]
```

Downstream — `wf-development` (decide *how*, build & verify) — reuses `tdd-cycle`, `verify-change`, `code-reviewer`/`security-reviewer`, `pr-review`, and the `csdd spec` gates; the `dev-*` skills add only the planning layer:

```text
1. dev-architecture     # architecture.md + ADRs → fills & approves design.md
2. dev-epics-stories    # epics.md + stories     → fills & approves tasks.md
3. dev-readiness-check  # PASS/CONCERNS/FAIL — required before the first tdd-cycle
4. dev-sprint           # sprint-status.yaml — live task tracking
5. tdd-cycle (existing) # one task at a time, red→green→refactor
6. code-reviewer / pr-review (existing) # resolve blockers before the next task
7. dev-retrospective    # retrospective.md → fold durable lessons into steering
```

Use this pairing for net-new products where the requirements do not yet exist; use the standard single-spec flow (11.2) when you already know what to build and only need the contract.

